# What never enters the build context.
#
# The Containerfile COPYs by name (Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, src/,
# rust-toolchain.toml), so this file is not what keeps the image clean — it is
# what keeps the CONTEXT from being sent at all. `target/` alone is gigabytes,
# and the build engine reads the whole context before the first instruction
# runs. `corpus/` and `lcov.info` are the other two of any size here.
#
# `.dockerignore` IS A SYMLINK TO THIS FILE, and that is one home rather than
# two. podman reads `.containerignore` and falls back to `.dockerignore`;
# docker reads only `.dockerignore` and has never heard of this name — so a
# `make image ENGINE=docker`, which the Makefile advertises, sent `.git/` and
# every byte of `target/` (tens of gigabytes in a working checkout) to the
# daemon before the symlink existed. Two copies of an ignore list drift within
# a week; a link cannot.
target/
.git/
# The corpus's fixtures stay out; its standing record `corpus/shapes.json` is
# a build input the Containerfile COPYs by name (bl-d58a). The siblings are
# named rather than the directory excluded and the file re-included, because
# a `!` exception under an excluded directory is honoured by neither engine
# reliably.
corpus/reply/
corpus/request/
corpus/README.md
lcov.info
